  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest analysis shows the surface low centered just north of\nToronto, Canada with the mid-level shortwave diving SE out of\nnorthern MI. This is resulting in warm front induced scattered\nthunderstorms firing in northwest PA (mostly into CTP's forecast\narea) while growing storm coverage approaches eastern OH ahead\nof the mid-level wave and surface front.\n\nConfidence is fairly high in the shortwave and pre-frontal\ntrough traversing SE through 12z/8am to kick off mostly \nelevated thunderstorms tapping 1000-1500 J/kg MUCAPE. The lack \nof surface based storms and stronger buoyancy should limit \nsevere potential but risks are non-zero thanks to near 1000 J/kg\nDCAPE that could foster strong enough descending air associated\nwith the most organized cells to result in localized damaging \nwinds. Increasing effective shear with the shortwave may also \nallow for enough updraft organization/maintenance to foster \nisolated instances of large hail. The biggest caveat is the \nlikely southward path the cluster is favored to take by hi-res \nmodels, meaning far eastern OH zones are the most at-risk for \nthis round of storm risks; areas east are likely to see more\nstratiform region rain showers and/or cloud cover the initially\ninhibits additional convective activity.\n\nThough the shortwave exits by 14z, additional lift offered in\ncooler NW flow plus the cross surface cold front should result\nin additional afternoon convection mainly east of I-77. Buoyancy\nmay recover near 1000 J/kg with enough DCAPE available to\nmaintain a damaging wind threat, assuming enough clearing in\nclouds occurs prior to frontal passage (not a given). Loss of\ndiurnal heating and waning upper support results in dwindling\nstorm activity after 21z tonight, with all risks tapering off by\n00z.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper pattern features a closed upper trough meandering over\nthe New England region late tonight through Thursday. Weak\nshortwave movement around this low plus wrap-around moisture\ncould foster isolated to scattered diurnal storms Wednesday\n(east of I-77) and Thursday (mainly along higher terrain) in\nwhat otherwise would be a drying period with slowly building\nhigh pressure.\n\nThese storms are unlikely to pose risks due to limited\ninstability (less than 500 J/kg).",
